# Copy to .dev.vars for local development (.dev.vars is gitignored).
# In production set these under
#   Workers & Pages -> visionguard -> Settings -> Variables and Secrets
# as ENCRYPTED secrets, not plaintext variables.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. Signs session cookies and peppers password hashes.
# 32+ random characters. Generate one:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Changing it signs everyone out AND invalidates every stored password hash,
# so existing users would have to reset. Set it once and leave it alone.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SESSION_SECRET=""

# Raise on the Workers PAID plan; the 25,000 default exists to stay inside the
# Free plan's 10 ms CPU budget. Existing hashes keep working either way.
# PBKDF2_ITERATIONS="210000"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google sign-in — NOTHING TO SET HERE. Deliberately.
#
# There is no GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET in this project and there should never be
# one. "Sign in with Google" is wired up with the ID-token flow: the browser
# gets an assertion signed by Google, and functions/api/auth/google.js verifies
# that signature against Google's published public keys. A client secret is
# only needed for the authorization-code flow, which this does not use.
#
# The client ID is public — it ships in the page on every site that offers
# Google sign-in — and lives in public/google-auth.js so the browser and the
# server read the same value. The server compares it against each token's
# `aud` claim, which is what stops a token minted for someone else's app from
# working here. If you ever change the client ID, change it there, once.
#
# What DOES need doing, in the Google Cloud console for this client:
#   Authorised JavaScript origins -> https://visionguardeg.com
#                                    (and http://localhost:5173 for local dev)
# No redirect URI is required for this flow.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Administrators — OPTIONAL.
#
# An admin account is a staff account that can additionally read EVERY
# employee's attendance (the Team tab on /account, and /api/attendance/team).
# It grants nothing else.
#
# Two ways to be one: role='admin' on the users row, or the address listed
# here. The default, with this unset, is the single address
# admin@visionguardeg.com. Comma-separated for more than one.
#
# Addresses on this list CANNOT be registered through the public signup form —
# an admin account is created out of band:
#
#   bash/zsh:    SESSION_SECRET="secret" npm run admin:create -- --password "..."
#   PowerShell:  $env:SESSION_SECRET = "secret"
#                npm run admin:create -- --password "..."
#
# It must be the deployment's own SESSION_SECRET: passwords are peppered with
# it, so a hash built with a different one can never be signed into.
# ADMIN_EMAILS="admin@visionguardeg.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Order notifications - ALL OPTIONAL.
#
# With none of this set, orders are still saved and the customer still gets a
# one-tap "send this order on WhatsApp" link on the confirmation screen. These
# variables only add the automatic push to your phone.
#
# Pick ONE provider. Telegram is the recommended one and wins automatically
# when TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN is set. See lib/whatsapp.js for the trade-offs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -- Option 0 (recommended): Telegram bot ------------------------------------
# Free, official, no template approval and no 24-hour sending window: the full
# multi-line Arabic summary arrives exactly as composed.
#
#   1. Message @BotFather on Telegram, /newbot, copy the token it gives you.
#   2. Open your new bot and send it any message (a bot cannot message you
#      first). For a group, add the bot to the group instead.
#   3. Find the chat id:
#        curl "https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates"
#      and read result[].message.chat.id — your own user id for a direct
#      message, or a negative id like -1001234567890 for a group.
#
# The token is a secret: anyone holding it controls the bot. Store it as an
# encrypted secret, never in wrangler.toml or a committed file, and /revoke it
# in @BotFather if it is ever pasted somewhere it should not have been.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=""        # e.g. 8961198092:AA...
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=""          # e.g. 123456789 or -1001234567890
#
# Leaving TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID empty still works: the code falls back to reading
# the most recent chat from getUpdates. That only sees the last 24 hours and
# returns nothing once a webhook is set on the bot, so set it once you know it.

# --- WhatsApp options -------------------------------------------------------
# Where the WhatsApp notification goes (ignored by Telegram, which uses
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID). Defaults to the number published on the site.
#
# THIS IS THE BACK OFFICE'S NUMBER, NOT THE CUSTOMER'S. It is routinely set to
# whichever phone is on the desk. Customers are never sent here — the
# pending-order screen and the order list use PUBLIC_WHATSAPP below.
WHATSAPP_TO="201105006854"

# The number CUSTOMERS are sent to: the "contact us on WhatsApp to pay" button
# on the pending-order screen, and the same button in their order history.
# Defaults to 201105006854, the number published in the strip, the menu, both
# footers and the assistant's answers. Only set this to a number the shop is
# happy to publish — and if you change it, change the site's copy with it.
# PUBLIC_WHATSAPP="201105006854"

# -- Option 1: Meta WhatsApp Cloud API (official, free tier) -----------------
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=""            # permanent access token from a System User
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID=""         # Phone number ID, NOT the phone number
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE=""         # approved template name with a single {{1}} body param
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_LANG="ar"
# WHATSAPP_ALLOW_TEXT="1"      # send plain text instead of a template (only valid
#                              # inside a 24h window - see lib/whatsapp.js)

# -- Option 2: UltraMsg (unofficial bridge, no template approval) ------------
# ULTRAMSG_INSTANCE=""         # e.g. instance12345
# ULTRAMSG_TOKEN=""

# -- Option 3: Twilio WhatsApp ----------------------------------------------
# TWILIO_SID=""
# TWILIO_TOKEN=""
# TWILIO_FROM="14155238886"    # your Twilio WhatsApp sender, digits only

# -- Option 4: CallMeBot (free, one recipient, plain text) -------------------
# CALLMEBOT_KEY=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Meta Conversion API (server-side, no secret in source code)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# META_PIXEL_ID=""           # your Meta Pixel ID, e.g. 123456789012345
# META_DATASET_ID=""         # optional Meta dataset ID, e.g. 37444427775716864
# META_ACCESS_TOKEN=""       # secret access token for CAPI requests
# META_CURRENCY="EGP"        # optional currency code for purchase events
# META_TEST_EVENT_CODE=""    # optional debugging code from Events Manager
# META_ATTRIBUTION_SHARE="0.3"  # optional attribution share for the event

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Marketing tab — READING from Meta. ALL OPTIONAL.
#
# The opposite direction to everything above. Those variables let the site
# SEND conversions; these let it READ Page, Instagram and Ads insights for the
# Marketing tab on /account. With none of them set the tab still appears for
# staff and says which of these is missing — it never shows a zero it cannot
# stand behind.
#
# EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS FILE NEEDS A REDEPLOY AFTER YOU SET IT IN THE
# DASHBOARD. Pages binds variables to a DEPLOYMENT, not to the project, so a
# secret added today is invisible to a deployment made yesterday — the
# dashboard shows it set and the site behaves as though it is missing.
# Cloudflare's own wording is that secrets must be set "before a deployment
# that uses those secrets". After adding anything here:
#
#   npx wrangler pages deploy --branch=main
#
# (--branch=main matters: this repo sits on `master` and the Pages production
# branch is `main`, so the plain command publishes a preview and the live
# site keeps serving the old build.)
#
# WHY A SEPARATE TOKEN. META_ACCESS_TOKEN above is a Conversions API token.
# It can send events and it CANNOT read insights — Graph answers those calls
# with an OAuth scope error. Reading needs five permissions the CAPI token
# does not carry:
#
#   pages_read_engagement, read_insights          Facebook Page
#   instagram_basic, instagram_manage_insights    Instagram
#   ads_read                                      Ad account
#
# Use a SYSTEM USER token, not a personal one. A personal token expires in
# about 60 days, and a dashboard wired to one works for two months and then
# goes blank on an ordinary morning with nothing in the logs to explain it. A
# System User token with the Page, the Instagram account and the ad account
# assigned to it does not expire. It also usually avoids App Review entirely:
# review governs asking OTHER people for these permissions, and this is your
# business reading its own assets.
#
#   Business Settings -> Users -> System Users -> Add
#     -> assign the Page, the Instagram account and the ad account
#     -> Generate Token with the five scopes above
#
# PREREQUISITE NO TOKEN CAN FIX: the Instagram account must be a Business or
# Creator account linked to the Page. A personal account has no insights over
# the API at all. Confirm the link with:
#   GET /{page-id}?fields=instagram_business_account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FOUR VARIABLES, NOT SIX. Older notes in this repository asked for
# META_PAGE_TOKEN and META_APP_SECRET as well. Both were for the Meta social
# inbox, which was REMOVED at the owner's request — no code reads either name
# any more (grep for META_ across lib/ and functions/ if you want to check).
# Setting them does nothing. Only the four below matter.
#
# META_INSIGHTS_TOKEN=""     # System User token with the five read scopes
# META_PAGE_ID=""            # defaults to 843967908810641 — see below
# META_IG_USER_ID=""         # the id from instagram_business_account above
# META_AD_ACCOUNT_ID=""      # defaults to act_2067738330681838 — see below
#
# AND REALLY TWO NOW: THE TOKEN, AND INSTAGRAM.
#
# Two of those four ids no longer need setting at all. They were read off the
# Vision Guard Eg business through the Meta developer tooling and are
# defaulted in lib/insights.js, for the same reason META_PIXEL_ID is
# defaulted in lib/meta.js: this project's wrangler.toml [vars] have not
# reliably reached the runtime, and an unset id there does not fail loudly —
# it renders "not configured" about a Page whose id has been known all along.
#
#   Page              843967908810641        "Vision Guard"
#   Ad account        act_2067738330681838   "vision guard", EGP, active
#   Business          843979858809446        "Vision Guard Eg"
#
# Set the variables anyway to point at something else; an explicit value
# always wins.
#
# INSTAGRAM IS THE ONE ID THAT COULD NOT BE RESOLVED. The ad account has no
# Instagram account linked for advertising, so it appears in no listing, and
# a guess here would point the tab at a stranger's profile. Get it from the
# Page and set it:
#
#   GET /843967908810641?fields=instagram_business_account
#
# If that returns nothing, the Instagram account is not a Business or Creator
# account linked to the Page, and no token can fix that — convert and link it
# in the Instagram app first. Until it is set, the Instagram panel says it is
# missing, which is true.
#
# THE TOKEN IS THE REAL WORK, and insightsConfig() falls back to
# META_ACCESS_TOKEN, which production already has for the Conversions API. So
# the tab ANSWERS today and will very likely return empty ads and page
# sections, because the CAPI token is a WRITE token and carries none of the
# read scopes. That is the failure the `dedicatedToken` flag in
# insightsStatus() exists to name: configured, answering, and empty. Generate
# the System User read token and set META_INSIGHTS_TOKEN to fix it properly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Catalogue API — PUSHING products into the Meta catalogue
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A third direction, and a third credential. lib/meta.js writes conversions,
# lib/insights.js reads Page and Ads numbers, lib/metacatalog.js writes
# PRODUCTS — and Meta scopes all three separately:
#
#   lib/meta.js       POST /{dataset}/events       a Conversions API token
#   lib/insights.js   GET  /{page}/insights        the five read scopes
#   lib/metacatalog.js POST /{catalog}/items_batch  catalog_management
#
# This drives POST /api/admin/meta-catalog. Without it the admin panel says
# so and /api/admin/export?kind=catalog still produces the .xlsx to upload by
# hand — that path is unchanged and is the fallback that always works.
#
# META_CATALOG_TOKEN=""      # System User token carrying catalog_management
# META_CATALOG_ID=""         # defaults to 1385708380173785 — see below
#
# THERE ARE TWO CATALOGUES ON THIS BUSINESS AND ONLY ONE IS RIGHT. Picking
# the wrong one is silent: products upload cleanly into a catalogue no ad
# reads.
#
#   1385708380173785   "VisonGuardEg-Cataogue"   <- the shop's, the default
#   1411420710903781   "CCTV"                     an older separate catalogue
#
# ONE TOKEN CAN SERVE BOTH THIS AND THE MARKETING TAB. metacatalog.js reads
# META_CATALOG_TOKEN and falls back to META_INSIGHTS_TOKEN, so a single
# System User token generated with catalog_management AND the five read
# scopes covers everything — one thing to rotate rather than two. It does NOT
# fall back to META_ACCESS_TOKEN: a Conversions API token authenticates and
# then cannot write a product, and that error reads like a broken integration
# rather than like missing configuration.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Meta app behind all of this: 1620559926365637 "Catalogue & Marketing"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owner: mahmoudnabil03@gmail.com. Currently in Development mode, which is
# the correct state for everything above and is deliberately being left that
# way — see the checklist below for what publishing would and would not
# change.
#
# WHY DEVELOPMENT MODE IS ENOUGH. App Review governs asking OTHER PEOPLE for
# permissions. Everything this project does is one business reading and
# writing its OWN assets, through a System User token issued by that
# business. That path does not require Advanced Access and does not require
# the app to be Live.
#
# WHAT THE OWNER STILL HAS TO DO BY HAND — none of it is possible from code:
#
#   1. Business Settings -> Accounts -> Apps -> Add
#        add app 1620559926365637 to business 843979858809446, or a System
#        User cannot generate a token for it at all.
#   2. Business Settings -> Users -> System Users -> Add
#        assign: the Page (843967908810641), the ad account
#        (2067738330681838) and the catalogue (1385708380173785).
#   3. Generate Token, against app 1620559926365637, with:
#        pages_read_engagement, read_insights      Page
#        instagram_basic, instagram_manage_insights Instagram
#        ads_read                                   Ads
#        catalog_management                         Catalogue
#   4. Put it in the Pages dashboard as META_INSIGHTS_TOKEN (and, if you
#      prefer a separate one for writes, META_CATALOG_TOKEN).
#   5. Redeploy. Pages binds variables to a DEPLOYMENT, not to a project:
#        npx wrangler pages deploy --branch=main
#
# USE A SYSTEM USER TOKEN, NOT A PERSONAL ONE. A personal token expires in
# about 60 days, and a dashboard wired to one works for two months and then
# goes blank on an ordinary morning with nothing in the logs to explain it.
#
# PUBLISHING THE APP IS THE OWNER'S CALL AND IS NOT NEEDED FOR ANY OF THIS.
# Switching it Live is what would let people OUTSIDE the business grant it
# permissions, and it first requires a privacy policy URL, a data deletion
# callback and business verification — none of which are set today. Nothing
# in this repository asks for it.


# Force a provider instead of auto-detecting from whichever keys are present.
# One of: telegram, meta, ultramsg, twilio, callmebot.
# NOTIFY_PROVIDER="telegram"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SITE_ORIGIN — the canonical origin the catalogue feed builds its URLs on
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. /api/admin/export?kind=catalog writes an image_link and a link for
# every product, and it defaults to whatever host the administrator was on
# when they pressed the button.
#
# That default is right locally and usually right in production, but the live
# site answers on BOTH visionguardeg.com and www.visionguardeg.com and neither
# redirects to the other. Meta stores the feed's `link` as the product's URL
# and matches it against what the pixel reports, so exporting from one host
# one week and the other the next quietly creates two of every product.
#
# Set this to whichever one is canonical for the shop and the question stops
# depending on how somebody navigated. No trailing slash.
# SITE_ORIGIN="https://visionguardeg.com"
